WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.330
Disorienting dreamscapes, leather daddies,
and a nod to ‘A Christmas Carol’?

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:11.830
The ending of American Horror Story: NYC is
a sad but hopeful culmination of a harrowing season.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:16.369
The first eight episodes of "American Horror
Story: NYC" focus more on mystery and crime

00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:18.699
than any previous season of the show.

00:00:18.699 --> 00:00:22.080
Episode 8, "Fire Island," is a particularly
action-packed example.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.240
The Mai Tai Killer is done and dusted, but
Big Daddy and Sam are up to no good.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.119
It all culminates in what might be the end
of Big Daddy, and the heartbreaking demise

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:31.360
of Theo Graves.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.780
The final moments of the episode see the show
enter a dark dreamscape, from which it never

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.100
fully emerges.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:44.630
The season's final episodes, "Requiem 1981/1987
Part One" and "Requiem 1981/1987 Part Two,"

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:48.680
proceed to dispense with anything resembling
dramatic tension, instead closing the show

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.010
with trippy dream sequences that bring the
Ghost of Christmas Future from "A Christmas

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:53.990
Carol" to mind.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:56.059
These dreams are far from sweet.

00:00:56.059 --> 00:01:00.879
No one gets a happy ending here, even if they
get a living one, and it's all a bit elliptical.

00:01:00.879 --> 00:01:05.430
But what the final episodes lack in narrative
drive, they make up for in their steadfast

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:10.300
commitment to portraying the full devastation
and stigmatization of the AIDS crisis.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.509
“This is supposed to be open, godd--n it!

00:01:14.509 --> 00:01:17.090
We want people to face what happened to you.”

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:22.210
"American Horror Story: NYC" finishes with
a dream-sequence dirge, but it starts as a

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.329
sexy, scary crime thriller.

00:01:24.329 --> 00:01:28.370
Audiences are kept guessing as to who or what
is killing Greenwich Village's gay men, and

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:32.890
if Patrick Read will figure out the answer
before the murderer sets his sights on him.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:37.240
As Patrick's actor, Russell Tovey, recounted
to Variety, series co-creator Ryan Murphy

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:37.840
told him:

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.420
As savvy viewers might know already, this
is a very intriguing comparison that highlights

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.640
one of the finale's most important threads.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:54.240
"Cruising" is a 1980 film starring Al Pacino
as a straight detective who goes undercover

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.320
as a gay man.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.360
The film's debut was met with controversy.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.420
As The New York Times reported, it even inspired
a march through Greenwich Village.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.500
Gay rights activist Ethan Geto spoke for many
when he decried the film's portrayal of gay

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.140
men and gay culture as dangerously demeaning.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:12.310
"American Horror Story: NYC" tackles this
legacy in a complex way.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:16.190
The fact that it isn't written from a straight
perspective makes a big difference: Patrick

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:19.810
is a gay man struggling with shame, rather
than an undercover straight man.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:25.270
It all comes crashing down on him in the finale,
which takes the season far from its more straightforward beginnings.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:31.140
What results is a fascinating continuation
and reversal of the legacy left behind by "Cruising."

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:36.260
Despair ties both halves of season 11 together,
and constitutes a main thread of the final

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:37.260
two episodes.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.670
It's sorrowful and rage-inducing to see Gino
Barelli write, protest, caretake, and ultimately

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.810
watch his loved ones die from AIDS.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:48.879
The fact that he does so in the face of poor
conditions and near-total public indifference

00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:50.680
makes things even more intense.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:56.630
Kathy Pizazz, the bathhouse chanteuse-slash-tarot-shop-proprietor,
laments to Adam Carpenter that the time has

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:58.630
come to close up shop, saying:

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:00.700
"All that music.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.040
All that love.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.940
Freedom incarnate.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.540
Vitality without shame.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:09.900
But it's gone now.”

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:14.350
This sentiment is in contrast to the way the
series usually paints these spaces.

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:18.311
This is, after all, a horror story, and though
people have fun in these clubs and bars, they

00:03:18.311 --> 00:03:20.159
also suffer and die.

00:03:20.159 --> 00:03:24.700
But in this moment, "American Horror Story:
NYC" chooses to frame them as wonderful places

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.080
brimming with joy.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.450
Despite the utter hopelessness and rage of
the season's ending, Kathy insists Adam maintain

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:33.820
hope and remember to live as long as he's
able.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:39.530
"American Horror Story: NYC" is full of sinister
acts, but it also explores heart-rending passion

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:40.860
and true love.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.270
Take Gino and Patrick.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:46.060
Despite their hardships, their bond lasts
all the way through the devastation of Patrick's

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:47.060
final days.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.989
Together, they move through shame to the safety
of their love.

00:03:49.989 --> 00:03:53.640
This is at the core of the season as a whole,
and the finale specifically.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.920
One of the most powerful moments of "American
Horror Story: NYC" happens when Gino confronts

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.610
Patrick about sleeping with other men and
being into the leather scene, which Patrick

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:03.800
previously claimed ignorance of.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:08.230
"You only tell the truth when you're about
to be caught.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:11.189
That – That's shame."

00:04:11.189 --> 00:04:14.760
Gino's words hit Patrick — and the audience
— square in the chest.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:19.530
For all his strength and swagger, Patrick
needs to be challenged and comforted by Gino.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:24.610
Patrick's growth, in turn, forces Gino to
consider a truly wild possibility: hope.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:29.120
When Gino comforts Patrick after an attempt
on his life, he insists they're safe.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.889
“You’re safe, you’re safe.”

00:04:31.889 --> 00:04:33.270
“I’m not.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.740
And we’re not safe, Gino.”

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:40.580
There's a lot of sad truth to this statement,
but it's not entirely accurate.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:44.730
In this moment, these two find solace in each
other, and that means everything.

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:48.680
In fact, in the world of "American Horror
Story," it's basically a miracle.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:53.510
The relationship between Adam and Theo Graves
is one of the most poignant elements of "American

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.229
Horror Story: NYC."

00:04:55.229 --> 00:04:59.271
It's sweet, sexy, and healthy — and because
this is "American Horror Story," it's not

00:04:59.271 --> 00:05:00.810
long for this world.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:02.760
Adam and Theo are something of an odd couple.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:07.160
The former is a square satisfied with nights
in, until he dedicates his hours to searching

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.030
for lost friends and advocating for his community.

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:14.510
The latter is creative, drawn to dark energy,
and finds beauty in surprising places.

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:19.080
Theo is also coming off of Sam's manipulative
machinations, which makes the steady energy

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.410
between him and Adam especially welcome.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:24.040
But then, Adam and Theo are cruelly parted.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:29.090
Adam, who has lost countless friends to possibly
the Mai Tai Killer and definitely Big Daddy,

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:34.410
now loses Theo to Sam, the biggest villain
on the show who isn't an allegory for illness.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:39.180
Theo's one-two punch of a demise via disease,
drugs, and possible sexual assault is the

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.830
traumatic cherry on top of an already stuffed-with-trauma
cake.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:46.880
The final episodes feature Theo in the role
of Sam's Afterlife Guide, which serves as

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.550
a brutal reminder to the audience that the
good guys definitely don't always win.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:56.490
Whether you think Patrick Read is toxic trash
or a guy just doing his best, one thing is

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.210
for sure: No one deserves such a rough death.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:03.160
For all his brutality and bluster, Patrick
wants to stop the bad guys — even when he

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:04.600
turns out to be one of them.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:09.560
Thanks to Gino's good influence, Patrick goes
from being a closeted cop to an out gay man.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.850
He still struggles with shame and silence,
but hey, it's progress!

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:16.360
The fact that Patrick takes the trophy for
the season's Most Improved Player makes his

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:18.110
death especially gutting.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:19.860
What rubs even more salt in the wound?

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.040
His Afterlife Guide is Barbara, his ex-wife,
who would have preferred Patrick to continue

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.280
living a lie.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:27.360
She walks him through the worst moments of
his life.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.710
This begs a simple question: why?

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:32.110
Are we to believe Patrick deserved his suffering?

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:33.950
Was he helpless to break the cycle of pain?

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:38.530
Did his own shame condemn him to spend the
afterlife reliving the pain he caused and

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.920
the pain the hateful world caused him?

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:43.530
It's unclear — and that might be the point.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:45.000
“The shame.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:46.010
The lies.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:46.829
The violence.

00:06:48.269 --> 00:06:49.759
It’s a cycle, Patrick.”

00:06:49.759 --> 00:06:53.580
Gino Barelli is one of the good guys, and
boy, is he mad about it.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:56.129
He's mad about everything, actually, and with
good reason.

00:06:56.129 --> 00:07:01.419
Not only is AIDS devastating his community,
Gino is almost murdered about a hundred times.

00:07:01.419 --> 00:07:04.599
But Gino just won't die — for a while, anyway.

00:07:04.599 --> 00:07:06.110
He has too much to do.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:12.220
Gino appears in both parts of "Requiem 1981/1987,"
and both appearances are heartbreaking examples

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:16.169
of his tenacity in the face of relentless
ignorance and cruelty.

00:07:16.169 --> 00:07:18.889
They're also proof of his deep and unending
love.

00:07:18.889 --> 00:07:23.110
His tenderness towards Patrick in his poor
excuse for a hospital room is particularly

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:27.949
vivid: He's not at his typewriter or a protest
site, but Gino's care is just as passionate

00:07:27.949 --> 00:07:29.129
and committed.

00:07:29.129 --> 00:07:33.660
This man's home fires are fueled by love and
rage, and he keeps both burning until the

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:34.660
bitter end.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:39.830
Sam is the embodiment of self-centeredness
and a horrifying example of just how reckless

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:43.190
and ruthless people can be when they only
care about what they want.

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:47.669
Though he's not technically a serial killer,
Sam does the dirtiest deeds on all of "American

00:07:47.669 --> 00:07:49.310
Horror Story: NYC."

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:52.530
"Look, I'm really sorry that I forgot my violin
for this perfect little moment.

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:55.129
but we do have a body to dispose of, so do
you want to pitch in?"

00:07:55.129 --> 00:08:00.069
In other words, Sam is a capital-V Villain
and the finale plays with this fact in fascinatingly

00:08:00.069 --> 00:08:01.630
disorienting ways.

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:07.419
"Requiem 1981/1987 Part One" is largely dedicated
to Theo's spirit guiding Sam through the terrible

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:11.750
acts he committed in life, as well as a very
creepy interlude with Sam's father, who caused

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:13.229
him significant pain.

00:08:13.229 --> 00:08:16.379
This episode is disorienting, due to its dreamlike
nature.

00:08:16.379 --> 00:08:21.080
Such an approach, paired with a deeper look
into Sam's psyche, packs a significant punch.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:24.610
While this might seem like a jarring switch
of storytelling and perspective, the sheer

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:28.949
amount of time the audience is forced to spend
in Sam's nightmare ends up working.

00:08:28.949 --> 00:08:32.669
We see a different side of Sam mere moments
before he makes out with his own personal

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:35.409
Big Daddy and catches a ride to the flip side.

00:08:35.409 --> 00:08:36.370
Does it redeem him?

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:37.170
Not really.

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:39.511
But it forces us into a new point of view.

00:08:39.971 --> 00:08:44.660
If "American Horror Story: NYC" is successful
at anything, it's making audiences understand

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:48.420
that great pain, shame, and desire drive drastic
actions.

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:52.830
The final episodes of "American Horror Story:
NYC" are a festival of death — and not just

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:53.830
the slasher kind.

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:59.180
The slow, silent march into oblivion is evidenced
by countless skull-masked men dropping into

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:04.850
an open grave, and Gino living out his final
days under Big Daddy's impenetrable, leather-clad gaze.

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:09.339
While this imagery is devastating, poetic,
and nearly overwhelming, it might also feel

00:09:09.339 --> 00:09:11.370
a bit confused to some viewers.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:14.850
Why, they might wonder, is the specter of
death a leather daddy?

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:17.660
What's the real-world meaning of this complicated
symbol?

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:22.070
The leather scene is important to the community
captured in "American Horror Story: NYC."

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:23.580
It's also full of pain.

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:27.710
Making Big Daddy the avatar of the AIDS crisis
can be seen as underscoring many points.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:32.380
He is a terrible perversion of community joy,
a symbol of the public's callousness and cruelty

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:36.180
towards gay men, and a complicated mingling
of anger and despair.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:40.760
Big Daddy's ending points towards deeper shame
still being teased out and resolved in the

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:42.450
aftermath of the AIDS crisis.

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:47.450
It's up to interpretation, but one thing is
clear: Big Daddy is key to the ending's meaning.

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:52.110
Adam Carpenter is the closest thing "American
Horror Story" has to a beacon of hope, so

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:54.459
it's a good thing he's the last man standing.

00:09:54.459 --> 00:09:56.649
Yet Adam still endures unspeakable heartbreak.

00:09:56.649 --> 00:10:02.349
He loses his lover, many friends, and experiences
the trauma of the Big Daddy and Mai Tai Killer attacks.

00:10:02.349 --> 00:10:04.780
Undaunted, Adam moves through his losses.

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:07.140
This momentum is key to the season's ending.

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:12.260
If Gino creates his own sentinel persona using
love, loss, and rage as fuel, Adam builds

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:17.400
his protector persona out of a dedication
to and deep understanding of his community.

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:21.160
He may look like a boy scout, but by the time
the finale rolls around, we know he's lived

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:22.220
through hell.

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:26.580
Adam's concerns about AIDS are consistently
dismissed, yet he still fights the good public

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:27.580
health fight.

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:30.600
It doesn't stop him, and it also doesn't break
him.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:34.560
Much lip service is paid to the importance
of transcending cycles of pain and protecting

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:38.420
one's community in the final episodes of "American
Horror Story: NYC."

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:40.170
Adam is shown doing just this.

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:44.149
By the time Adam stands to speak at Gino's
funeral, we see him open his mouth, take a

00:10:44.149 --> 00:10:46.200
breath — and that's it.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.850
We don't need more, because his actions speak
for themselves.

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:53.510
We already know that whatever Adam has to
say will provide his community with a much-needed

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:55.689
bit of light in the unrelenting dark.

